#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""secrets-admin — the ONLY entry point for the Cockpit secrets plugin.

Same house contract as wg-admin / adlab-admin / hs-admin (see
cockpit-wireguard/docs/CONTRACT.md and docs/CONTRACT.md here):

  - the plugin never runs raw commands; it calls this helper with a narrow verb
  - every verb prints ONE JSON object on stdout and NOTHING else
  - diagnostics, warnings and notes go to stderr
  - exit 0 = success; on failure stdout carries {"error": ..., "detail": ...}

with the one addition the subject matter forces:

  - **no verb accepts a secret as an argument.** The verb is on argv; every
    other input is ONE JSON object read from stdin, capped at 1 MiB (I10).

Install:  /usr/local/sbin/secrets-admin, mode 0755, root:root
Call as:  cockpit.spawn(["/usr/local/sbin/secrets-admin", VERB],
                        { superuser: "require" | null, err: "message" })
          ...then write the JSON request to the child's stdin and close it.

WHAT THIS FILE IS RESPONSIBLE FOR
---------------------------------
This is the security core. The format backends (backends/kdbx.py,
backends/psafe3.py) do the cryptography; everything that decides *whether an
operation may happen at all* lives here:

    request framing      one JSON object on stdin, never argv/env/tmp    I10
    registry loading     /etc/cockpit-secrets/safes.d/*.json, fail closed I1
    identity             geteuid/getuid/getgroups/SUDO_UID, never the body I2, I3
    the class gate       re-derived and re-checked inside EVERY verb      I3
    path resolution      a registry id -> a path; the ONLY place          I4
    file trust           delegated to base.open_safe_fd (O_NOFOLLOW+fstat) I5
    lockout              per-(REAL uid, safe) backoff, a per-safe rate
                         cap, and a constant time floor         I16, I39, I40
    audit                verb/safe/uid/outcome — never a value            I15
    export               four refusals, a helper-derived 0600 path        I21
    the agent client     opt-in, never started, never fatal               I18
    the barrier          no traceback, no locals, no value ever on stdout I15

DESIGN RULES, ALL LOAD-BEARING
------------------------------
  - **The `schema` verb is the single source of truth for the UI.** Every
    control, default, bound, option, placeholder, help string and
    `breaks_when_wrong` hint the plugin renders comes from `SCHEMA` below.
    Anything not emitted there cannot appear in the UI, by construction.
  - **A handle does not survive the process.** It is 128 bits from
    `secrets.token_urlsafe`, bound to (uid, safe id, pid), and lives in a
    module-global dict and nowhere else — not /run, not /var/lib, not a
    file. One `cockpit.spawn` per verb therefore means one unlock per verb,
    which is what makes "the passphrase is prompted every time" a property of
    the process tree rather than a policy someone can relax (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md).
  - **The multi-verb `open` session is the one documented exception**, and it
    is still one process: it reads newline-delimited request frames from stdin
    and writes one JSON response line each, so unlock -> edit -> save runs under
    a single unlock, and it ends when stdin closes, the idle timer fires or
    `lock` is called. Close the browser tab, the channel closes, the process
    dies, the unlock is over.
  - **The access class is enforced here, from kernel-supplied identity, on
    every verb** — never from the request body and never in the browser. A
    browser-side check is decoration; cockpit-guac-rdp's I4 proved it (I3).
  - **`admin` is the default access class.** A registry entry that omits
    `access` is admin; an entry that fails validation is DROPPED and reported
    through `health`, never partially applied and never defaulted open (I1).
  - **Nothing that leaves this file has been through anything but `redact()`.**
    Credentials are zeroed *before* a response is serialised, so the blanket
    filter over stdout is a safety net rather than a mangler of legitimate
    output (I15).
  - **A verb that creates a file derives its NAME here.** `export`, `save-as`
    and `restore-backup` are the three, and none of them accepts a path from a
    caller: an export's name is the safe id plus a UTC stamp, a save-as name is
    a single validated file name joined to the directory the registry already
    gave, and a restore names a generation by membership of the `backups`
    listing. Each writes its file name and, for export, its row count to the
    audit log — I21's "always audited by name" (I4, I21).
  - **`breach-check` has no network client, by construction.** There is no
    socket, no resolver and no URL anywhere on its path; an unconfigured corpus
    answers `available:false` and stops. A Cockpit page that phones out about
    the passwords it holds is precisely what this project must not become.

TEST SEAMS — and why they change no security property
-----------------------------------------------------
`COCKPIT_SECRETS_ETC` overrides the registry ROOT and `COCKPIT_SECRETS_VAR`
overrides the state/audit ROOT, so the test suite can run hermetically without
root. Neither is a hole:

  - both roots are re-checked on every run: absolute, a real directory, owned by
    root or by this euid, and not group- or other-writable;
  - a registry only ever *names* files. Every file is then opened by
    `base.open_safe_fd` with O_NOFOLLOW and validated by `fstat` on the fd
    against the expected uid and mode, so pointing a fabricated entry at
    /etc/shadow gets access-denied on ownership, not a read;
  - the class gate is unchanged: an `admin` entry still needs euid 0 plus group
    membership, and an unprivileged caller cannot manufacture either.

`COCKPIT_SECRETS_LIB` (where `backends/` lives) is deliberately NOT honoured
when euid is 0 — that one names *code*, and an env var that selects code in a
root process is a privilege escalation with extra steps.

`COCKPIT_SECRETS_DEBUG=1` allows a traceback on stderr — redacted, still never
on stdout, and off by default.

`COCKPIT_SECRETS_FAIL_FLOOR` lowers the I16 constant time floor so the suite is
not 0.75 s per negative case. It is IGNORED on the production admin path (euid 0
with the real registry), because that is the only configuration where the timing
distinction between "wrong passphrase" and "not even a valid attempt" is worth
anything to an attacker.

stdlib only at import time. `jsonschema` and the backends are optional and
imported lazily; a missing one is reported, never a traceback.

